		{"id":"8e39c92228f26d94","type":"text","text":"","x":-560,"y":821,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"a2e78345a9282fd4","x":376,"y":-220,"width":1024,"height":460,"type":"text","text":"**KEY = FEDERALISM:** struggle for recognition of  Indigenous land rights (Aboriginal title, e.g.) was bound up with struggle between provinces and federal government (in Canada) and between the states and federal government (U.S.)\n- this was not only the context for St. Catherine's Milling, but for for Wochester v Georgia -- where background was that Georgia was challenging the federal government's power to make/enforce treaties (Blackhawk p242)\n\nThis links closely with McLaren v Caldwell and the struggle between provinces and feds over powers of expropriation. \n\n\nThe focus on Indigenous resistance here is through E v Syliboy and the Mi'kmaq continued work to assert their treaty rights as the framework for an intern'll order \n\nNB: If the focus in unit 1 is about the indeterminism of property's origins, the focus in unit 2 is about the indeterminacy of positive law itself, paricularly in the form of feudal tenure and estates and the superstructure for land rights. What is title? Allodial title? Sovereignty? Treaty? "}
